{"model":"clef","state":"Symbol kind: Interface
Name: ModelDiff
Qualified name: src.core.virtual-projection.ModelDiff
File: src/core/virtual-projection.ts (lines 40-57)
Flags: exported
Docstring: /** * Model diff categorizing changeset changes by type. * Separates staged changes into additions (new elements), modifications (updated attributes), * and deletions (removed elements). Used for preview and diff display operations. */
Callers (0): none
Callees (0): none
File imports: src/core/model.ts, src/core/layer.ts, src/core/element.ts, src/types/index.ts, src/core/manifest.ts, src/core/relationships.ts, src/core/changeset.ts, src/core/staged-changeset-storage.ts

Source:
```
export interface ModelDiff {
  additions: Array<{
    elementId: string;
    layerName: string;
    data: Record<string, unknown>; // Complete element state (from change.after)
  }>;
  modifications: Array<{
    elementId: string;
    layerName: string;
    before: Record<string, unknown>; // Previous state (from change.before)
    after: Record<string, unknown>; // New state (from change.after)
  }>;
  deletions: Array<{
    elementId: string;
    layerName: string;
    data: Record<string, unknown>; // Element state before deletion (from change.before)
  }>;
}
```","questions":{"layer":{"type":"choice","instructions":"Which Documentation Robotics architecture layer would this code symbol be recorded in if it were modelled as an element? Choose 'none' if it is not an architecturally meaningful element on its own. Only the layers this kind of symbol can belong to are listed.","criteria":{"application":"Application Layer (ArchiMate 3.2): Models the application architecture: application components (deployable modules, classes and services), the application functions and processes that describe their internal behaviour, the application services and interfaces through which that behaviour is exposed, application events and interactions, collaborations between components, and the data objects they process (ArchiMate 3.2 application layer). This is the default home for ordinary application code structure; more specific layers apply only when a symbol is itself an API contract, a data schema, a persistence object, a UI element, telemetry, security logic or a test asset. Modelling rules: components and collaborations are assigned to the functions, processes and interactions they perform (assigned-to), and a collaboration aggregates its components and nested collaborations. A component provides the interfaces its own code declares and is assigned to an interface it only handles; an interface is assigned to the services it offers. Functions, processes and interactions realize application services; a component may realize one directly, as a shortcut through the behaviour assigned to it. Services serve the components, functions, processes and interactions that use them. Functions, processes, interactions, events and services access data objects, and so do components, unlike in the business layer, because a component is code that holds and passes data; interfaces and collaborations do not. Functions, components and events also access the data-model object schemas and schema definitions they read and write, such as entity classes, directly rather than through a data object. Components, functions and services likewise access the data-store collections and views they read and write. Events trigger processes, interactions and functions (unlike a business function, an application function can be an event handler), and behaviour raises events. A hand-off between behaviours is flows-to; depends-on means one element needs another to work, and is never used where assigned-to, accesses, serves or triggers says it. Across layers, application processes and functions realize the business processes and functions they automate, and application services realize business services and product features and capabilities and, with the behaviour that realizes them, carry the link to motivation (realize goals, satisfy requirements and constraints, deliver value). Example element types: ApplicationCollaboration, ApplicationComponent, ApplicationEvent, ApplicationFunction, ApplicationInteraction, ApplicationInterface, ApplicationProcess, ApplicationService.","none":"Not an architecturally meaningful element on its own: a trivial helper, formatting utility, boilerplate, generated code, test scaffolding, or an implementation detail below the level a 13-layer architecture model would record."}},"significance":{"type":"score","instructions":"How architecturally significant is this symbol within its codebase?","criteria":["0: implementation detail (private helper, formatter, trivial wrapper, constant)","1: minor internal unit that only its file cares about","2: notable internal unit a maintainer would name when explaining the module","3: architecturally significant (a service, component, contract, store, policy or integration point)","4: core architectural element: an entry point, system boundary, or central abstraction"]},"concern_authentication":{"type":"noul","instructions":"This symbol itself implements the authentication concern (verifies identity: login, tokens, API keys, sessions, credentials), not merely calling something that does."},"concern_authorization":{"type":"noul","instructions":"This symbol itself implements the authorization concern (decides what an identity may do: roles, permissions, policies, access checks), not merely calling something that does."},"concern_data_persistence":{"type":"noul","instructions":"This symbol itself implements the data persistence concern (reads or writes durable storage: files, databases, caches, manifests on disk), not merely calling something that does."},"concern_external_integration":{"type":"noul","instructions":"This symbol itself implements the external integration concern (calls or serves an external system or process: HTTP clients, MCP servers, subprocesses, third-party APIs), not merely calling something that does."},"concern_observability":{"type":"noul","instructions":"This symbol itself implements the observability concern (telemetry, logging, metrics, tracing, spans), not merely calling something that does."},"concern_user_interface":{"type":"noul","instructions":"This symbol itself implements the user interface concern (renders or drives a user-facing interface: CLI output, web UI, TUI, prompts), not merely calling something that does."}}}

