ject.","product":"Product Layer (ArchiMate 3.2 Strategy Extension + Product Management Practice): Frames the system from a product-management point of view: the personas it serves, the software capabilities it must possess, the features that deliver them, the user workflows that exercise them and the milestones that schedule delivery. It adapts the ArchiMate 3.2 strategy vocabulary rather than adopting it: a software capability is an ability of the software platform, not ArchiMate's Capability, which is the organization's ability and is modelled as business.businesscapability. Product elements describe what the software offers and to whom, never how it is built. Modelling rules: realization runs upward through product. Application and technology services realize software capabilities and features, views realize features, and navigation flows and flow steps realize user workflows and their steps; product elements in turn realize business behaviour, a software capability realizing the business function it carries out and a user workflow the business process the user performs. A feature realizes the software capabilities it exercises; software capabilities aggregate narrower capabilities, and capabilities and features may depend on their own kind. Features and user workflows serve the personas they are designed for; a persona describes a kind of person, so it references the stakeholder and business role it stands for rather than realizing or serving them. A user workflow composes its steps, steps flow to steps, and each step uses the features it exercises; a workflow reaches features and software capabilities only through its steps. Software capabilities support goals; software capabilities and features satisfy requirements; features deliver value and workflows deliver outcomes. Software capabilities and features are scheduled for milestones, and milestones precede milestones. Example element types: Feature, Milestone, Persona, SoftwareCapability, UserWorkflow, WorkflowStep.","security":"Security Layer (NIST SP 800-53): Defines how the system identifies, authorizes and protects: authentication configuration, roles, permissions, secure resources and their operations, access conditions and field-level controls, declarative policies and rules, data classification and rate limits, together with goal-oriented security concepts such as actors, objectives, delegations, threats, countermeasures and accountability (NIST SP 800-53 controls with STS-ml and XACML-style policy modelling). Code that checks identity or enforces permissions is evidence for elements here. Modelling rules: the security model composes its authentication configuration, constraints container and policies, and aggregates the shared elements (actors, roles, permissions, secure resources, information entities, delegations, threats, classifications and accountability requirements); a policy composes its rules, and a secure resource composes its operations and field access controls. Actors are assigned to roles, roles aggregate permissions and specialize a parent role, and permissions authorize resource operations; access follows that chain. An authentication configuration authenticates actors, uses its password policy and protects resources. A policy rule uses the conditions it evaluates, protects its target, authorizes permissions, triggers its policy actions and generates evidence; conditions and actions do not constrain or trigger on their own. Policies (security, password, retention and data classification) mandate the countermeasures they call for, and only countermeasures mitigate threats. A threat targets resources, information entities and business processes and services, influences classifications and policies, and references the motivation driver it gives rise to. Audit configurations, policy rules and delegations generate evidence; an audit configuration monitors resources and satisfies accountability requirements, and a retention policy governs the audit trails, evidence and information it keeps. A data classification governs the information entities, secure resources and retention policies it labels; field access controls are constrained by it, and data elements in other layers reference it. Separation of duty, binding of duty and need to know constrain roles, permissions and operations; the constraints container only groups them, and other layers link to the individual rule they follow. Validation rules constrain field values; they do not decide access. Example element types: AccessCondition, AccountabilityRequirement, Actor, AuditConfig, AuthenticationConfig, BindingOfDuty, Condition, Countermeasure.","application":"Application Layer (ArchiMate 3.2): Models the application architecture: application components (deployable modules, classes and services), the application functions and processes that describe their internal behaviour, the application services and interfaces through which that behaviour is exposed, application events and interactions, collaborations between components, and the data objects they process (ArchiMate 3.2 application layer). This is the default home for ordinary application code structure; more specific layers apply only when a symbol is itself an API contract, a data schema, a persistence object, a UI element, telemetry, security logic or a test asset. Modelling rules: components and collaborations are assigned to the functions, processes and interactions they perform (assigned-to), and a collaboration aggregates its components and nested collaborations. A component provides the interfaces its own code declares and is assigned to an interface it only handles; an interface is assigned to the services it offers. Functions, processes and interactions realize application services; a component may realize one directly, as a shortcut through the behaviour assigned to it. Services serve the components, functions, processes and interactions that use them. Functions, processes, interactions, events and services access data objects, and so do components, unlike in the business layer, because a component is code that holds and passes data; interfaces and collaborations do not. Functions, components and events also access the data-model object schemas and schema definitions they read and write, such as entity classes, directly rather than through a data object. Components, functions and services likewise access the data-store collections and views they read and write. Events trigger processes, interactions and functions (unlike a business function, an application function can be an event handler), and behaviour raises events. A hand-off between behaviours is flows-to; depends-on means one element needs another to work, and is never used where assigned-to, accesses, serves or triggers says it. Across layers, application processes and functions realize the business processes and functions they automate, and application services realize business services and product features and capabilities and, with the behaviour that realizes them, carry the link to motivation (realize goals, satisfy requirements and constraints, deliver value). Example element types: ApplicationCollaboration, ApplicationComponent, ApplicationEvent, ApplicationFunction, ApplicationInteraction, ApplicationInterface, ApplicationProcess, ApplicationService.","technology":"Technology Layer (ArchiMate 3.2): Models the infrastructure and platform the application runs on: nodes and devices, system software and runtimes, frameworks, communication networks and paths, technology services, interfaces, functions and processes, and the artifacts that are deployed to them (ArchiMate 3.2 technology layer). Package manifests, container images, deployment descriptors and infrastructure-as-code are the usual evidence. Modelling rules: nodes compose their devices, system software, interfaces and the artifacts deployed to them; nodes and system software are assigned to the functions and processes they perform, and a collaboration aggregates the nodes, system software and nested collaborations that cooperate in it and is assigned to its interactions. System software provides the interfaces it declares; an interface is assigned to the technology services it offers and serves the behaviour that uses it. Functions, processes and interactions realize technology services, and a node, system software, collaboration, path or network may realize one directly; a hand-off between behaviours is flows-to; events trigger behaviour, behaviour and services raise events, and structure does not trigger. Artifacts are passive: behaviour and system software access them, and they realize the system software they install and the application components they package, such as a container image. A communication network realizes the paths over it, and networks and paths connect the nodes and devices on them; other elements reach a network through the node they run on. Technology services serve nodes, other services and the application (components, services and functions); a node, device or system software may serve an application component directly as a shortcut for hosting it. Technology reaches the business layer through the application layer. Services carry the link to motivation: they realize goals and software capabilities and satisfy requirements; structure satisfies requirements, constraints and principles. Other layers reach technology in two ways: platform services serve the API, UX, APM, navigation and data-store elements that use them, and an element built on or run by a software product depends on that system software (a data-store database also depends on its node). Example element types: Artifact, CommunicationNetwork, Device, Node, Path, SystemSoftware, TechnologyCollaboration, TechnologyEvent.","api":"API Layer (OpenAPI 3.0): Describes the HTTP API surface the application exposes or consumes: the API document and its servers, paths and operations, parameters, request bodies and responses, 