ical data model: object schemas and their properties, enumerations, validation rules and the relationships between entities, independent of any particular storage technology (JSON Schema Draft 7). Entity classes, ORM models, DTO and record types, and type definitions that describe domain data are evidence for elements here; how that data is persisted belongs to the data-store layer. Modelling rules: data-model elements are passive structure; they are realized, accessed, referenced and mapped, and never serve, trigger or perform behaviour. A subschema written in place is part of what declares it (composes): a schema document composes its root schema and the definitions declared in it, an object schema composes its properties, an array schema its inline item schema, a composition its inline branches, and a property the value constraints it carries. A type declared elsewhere and reached by $ref or by name is referenced (references): a property references the object schema or definition its value takes, an array or composition references a named definition, and a Reference references the definition, object schema or document it points at. Subtyping is specializes. An entity class, an ORM model whatever the framework, or a type whose shape is published as a schema is an object schema; an in-code structure that only carries data between parts of the program is an application data object, which an object schema may realize. Object schemas and definitions realize the application data objects and business objects they give form to (a direct link to the business object is a shortcut through the data object), and map to their counterparts in other layers (maps-to): API schemas, request bodies, responses and parameters, and data-store collections. Behaviour reaches data by accessing it: application functions, components and events access object schemas and definitions, and business behaviour accesses the business objects they realize; a schema does not reference the behaviour that uses it. Schemas satisfy the requirements and constraints that govern the data but do not realize goals. The model is independent of technology: it does not depend on system software or use technology services, and the file that holds a schema document maps to a technology artifact. Example element types: ArraySchema, JSONSchema, NumericSchema, ObjectSchema, Reference, SchemaComposition, SchemaDefinition, SchemaProperty.","data-store":"Data Store Layer (ISO/IEC 9075 (SQL) + Paradigm Extensions): Describes physical persistence: databases and schemas, tables and collections, columns and fields, indexes, constraints, views, migrations, access patterns and the queries that read and write them (ISO/IEC 9075 SQL with extensions for document, key-value and graph paradigms). Migration scripts, table definitions and repository or DAO code that talks to storage are evidence for elements here. Modelling rules: containment is one level at a time: a database composes namespaces, or collections and views directly when the engine has none; a namespace composes collections, views, stored logic and nested namespaces; a collection composes its fields, indexes, validation rules and event handlers, and may compose partitions. Stored data is passive: collections, views, fields and databases are accessed, referenced and mapped, and never serve or trigger; application components, functions and services access the collections and views they read and write. Behaviour inside the store is stored logic, event handlers and access patterns: event handlers are attached to a collection, reference the fields they watch and trigger stored logic; stored logic accesses collections, triggers other stored logic and refreshes views; access patterns access a collection, use indexes and reference the fields in their key and filter conditions, and serve the application functions that issue them. Validation rules reference the fields they constrain, and a foreign key cascades to the collection it references. Views derive from collections and views; retention policies govern collections and namespaces, archive to collections and trigger the stored logic that purges them. Across layers, the store reaches the business through the application layer and the data model: stored logic realizes application functions, event handlers trigger application events, object schemas map to collections, and a collection may realize a business object as a shortcut; a view maps to the API response it backs. Example element types: AccessPattern, Collection, Database, EventHandler, Field, Index, Namespace, RetentionPolicy.","ux":"UX Layer (HTML 5.3): Models the user interface: views and screens, library components and their application-specific instances, forms, action and state patterns, component properties and the visual hierarchy they compose (HTML 5.3 vocabulary with component-library conventions). Rendered components, templates and view classes are evidence for elements here; the logic they call belongs to the application layer and the paths that reach them to the navigation layer. Modelling rules: placement is composition. A view composes its sub-views, component instances and action components; a sub-view or library sub-view composes its instances; an instance composes its nested instances, action components, slot references, chart series and table columns, and a menu composes its entries. A UX library aggregates its library components and sub-views, and a library component composes the components and slot references it is built from; an instance or placed sub-view realizes the library definition it places, and a slot reference references the component that fills it. Elements apply their layout configuration; state transitions, state patterns and action patterns use transition templates, which describe motion only. A state pattern aggregates its states; a state composes its outgoing transitions, a transition flows to its destination state, states and transitions trigger state actions, and a state action realizes its template. An action pattern governs the action components it is applied to. A view, sub-view, component instance or action component binds to its data configuration, which maps to the API or data-model data it reads; chart series and table columns bind through their component. Elements use their error configuration. A view realizes the application interface it presents and the product feature it delivers; an action component triggers the API operation or application behaviour it starts. UX does not access stores, operations or services directly, and reaches the business layer through the application interfaces and features it realizes: a view serves the business role it is for and references the business object it shows. A view supports the goals and outcomes it contributes to, and UX elements satisfy requirements and principles. Example element types: ActionComponent, ActionPattern, ChartSeries, ComponentInstance, ComponentReference, DataConfig, ErrorConfig, ExperienceState.","navigation":"Navigation Layer (SPA Navigation Patterns): Describes how a user moves through the application: routes and route parameters, navigation flows and their steps, guards and redirects, menus, deep links and the transitions between views (single-page-application navigation patterns). Router configuration and route tables are the usual evidence; the views reached belong to the UX layer and server-side HTTP endpoints to the API layer. Modelling rules: a navigation graph composes its routes and transitions and aggregates its flows, guards and breadcrumb configurations; a route composes its route meta and may contain child routes; a flow composes its steps and aggregates the context variables it carries. A transition leaves its source route (the route flows to it) and navigates to its destination route; a flow step navigates to its route, and a guard action redirects by navigating to a route. A guard evaluates its condition, triggers its action on den