t with and aggregate their processors and exporters; they configure telemetry and do not emit, serve or monitor it. An instrumentation configuration monitors the library or code it instruments and references the scope it emits under. Each signal flows through its own processors to exporters, and an exporter accesses the storage it writes to and is served by the technology service it sends to; signals of different kinds are correlated by references (a log record to its span, a metric's exemplars to spans, a span link to its span), not by flow. Only signals and instrumentation configurations monitor elements in other layers; alerts and dashboards monitor signals, and a dashboard references the alerts it shows. Telemetry reaches the business layer only through business KPIs, metric instruments that monitor business processes and services; a metric that tracks an outcome measures it, and telemetry satisfies requirements and constraints. Example element types: Alert, Dashboard, ExporterConfig, InstrumentationConfig, InstrumentationScope, LogConfiguration, LogProcessor, LogRecord.","testing":"Testing Layer (IEEE 829-2008): Describes how the system is verified: test strategies and plans, suites and cases, fixtures and test data, mocks and stubs, coverage targets and summaries, and the environments tests run in (IEEE 829-2008 test documentation). Test files, fixtures and test configuration are evidence for elements here; the production code under test belongs to the layer it implements. Modelling rules: each testing type has one composing parent. A test coverage model composes its coverage targets, input space partitions, context variations, environment factors, exclusions and coverage summary; a coverage target composes its target input fields, outcome categories, coverage requirements and test case sketches; a coverage requirement composes its input partition selections; a partition composes its values; a test case sketch composes its input selections. A test case sketch 