ilt, deployed, or replaced as a unit, such as a service, module, package, or dependency-injected class with its own responsibility. It is the thing that performs behaviour, not the behaviour itself. Use ApplicationFunction instead when the symbol is a unit of behaviour, such as a method, function, or handler body, that a component performs, rather than the structural unit that owns that behaviour. Use ApplicationInterface instead when the symbol is only the access point through which a component's services are reached, such as a controller endpoint set, client stub, or exported port, rather than the implementing unit. Use ApplicationCollaboration instead when the symbol represents a grouping of several components that work together, such as a system-of-services or a composite deployment, rather than a single replaceable unit. Use DataObject instead when the symbol is a passive data structure, such as a DTO, record, or entity without behaviour of its own, that components read or write. Evidence: a module, package, or service directory with its own build manifest or deployment descriptor (package.json, pom.xml, go.mod, Dockerfile, Kubernetes Deployment); a class annotated or registered as a managed component in a DI container (@Component, @Service, @Injectable, Module providers) whose instances are injected into other classes; an entry in docker-compose, Helm charts, or an architecture/service catalogue (e.g. Backstage catalog-info.yaml) naming the unit as a separately deployable service or library.","applicationevent":"ApplicationEvent: An application behavior element representing a named, instantaneous occurrence of a state change that an application component emits or receives, and that triggers or is consumed by other application behavior. It has no duration and performs no work itself; reacting is done by processes, functions or interactions. Use DataObject instead when the symbol is a data structure that components read, store or transform, and it is never published, emitted or subscribed to as a notification of something that happened. Use ApplicationProcess instead when the symbol is the handler, listener body or workflow that runs in response to the event, or that produces it, rather than the occurrence itself. Use ApplicationService instead when the symbol is an exposed capability that consumers invoke or subscribe to, such as an event stream offered as a service, rather than one specific kind of occurrence. Use ApplicationInteraction instead when the symbol describes the joint publish-subscribe or messaging behavior between two or more components rather than a single event type exchanged within it. Evidence: A class or record named in the past tense (e.g. OrderPlaced, UserRegisteredEvent) that extends a framework event base type such as Spring ApplicationEvent or a DomainEvent marker, and is passed to a publisher (eventPublisher.publish, mediator.Publish, bus.emit).; A string or constant event or topic name used in both an emit or publish call and a subscription (emitter.on('invoice.paid'), @EventListener, @KafkaListener(topics=...), RabbitMQ routing key).; A message entry in an AsyncAPI document, event catalog or CloudEvents type registry that declares the event's name and payload and lists its producers and consumers..","applicationinterface":"ApplicationInterface: An active-structure access point owned by an application component or collaboration, such as a mounted controller, gRPC server binding, GraphQL endpoint or message-channel listener, through which that component's application services are reachable by users or other components over a specific application-level protocol. It is the channel itself, not the behaviour offered through it and not the formal contract document describing it. Use ApplicationService instead when the element describes the externally visible behaviour or functionality offered, such as 'order placement', independent of the endpoint, protocol or channel through which it is reached. Use ApplicationComponent instead when the symbol is the deployable or replaceable module, service or package as a whole, rather than one of the access points it provides. Use ApplicationInteraction instead when the symbol represents joint behaviour carried out by two or more components exchanging messages, rather than the single access point one component offers. Use ApplicationFunction instead when the symbol is internal handler or business logic invoked behind the endpoint, rather than the endpoint binding or adapter itself. Evidence: A controller or router class that groups route handlers under a base path and is mounted on a web server, such as a Spring @RestController with @RequestMapping("/orders"), an Express Router passed to app.use('/orders', ...), or a FastAPI APIRouter included in the app.; A gRPC servicer or GraphQL schema/resolver set registered on a server at startup, such as add_OrderServiceServicer_to_server(...) or an Apollo server exposing /graphql.; A message or socket entry point bound to a named channel in code or config, such as a @RabbitListener(queues="orders") consumer, an AMQP queue binding in application.yml, or a WebSocket endpoint handler (@ServerEndpoint, socket.io namespace)..","applicationservice":"ApplicationService: An externally visible unit of application behavior that a component, function, process or interaction makes available to consumers (users, business processes, or other components) as a named set of operations, defined by what it offers rather than how it is implemented or accessed. Use ApplicationFunction instead when the behavior is internal to a component, such as a private helper, mapper or validation routine, and is not offered as a named capability to callers outside that component. Use ApplicationInterface instead when the symbol is the access point or binding through which a service is reached, such as an HTTP controller, gRPC endpoint, WebSocket handler or message listener, carrying a specific protocol. Use ApplicationComponent instead when the symbol is the deployable or replaceable structural unit (module, package, microservice, class as a unit of deployment) that performs or realizes behavior, rather than the behavior it exposes. Use ApplicationProcess instead when the symbol is an ordered sequence of steps, such as a saga, workflow or orchestrator method, that achieves a result rather than an offered capability. Evidence: A service interface or abstract contract, such as IOrderService or a gRPC 'service' block in a .proto file, whose operations are injected into or called from controllers, handlers or other modules; A class annotated @Service, @Injectable or registered in a DI container whose public methods are invoked across module or component boundaries rather than only within its own package; A queue consumer, scheduled batch job or event subscriber documented or configured to fulfil requests from other systems under a named capability, for example a 'payment-processing' worker bound in messaging or cron configuration.","dataobject":"DataObject: A passive application element: a code-level data structure (class, record, struct, type alias or message payload) whose purpose is to hold state that application components, functions and services create, read, transform or pass along, and which performs no application behavior beyond trivial accessors, constructors or self-validation. Use ApplicationComponent instead when the class encapsulates substantive behavior or dependencies (injected collaborators, business logic, I/O) and acts as a modular, replaceable unit rather than as a carrier of state. Use ApplicationEvent instead when the structure represents the occurrence of a state change that is published or dispatched to trigger reactive behavior, rather than data that is merely accessed; an event class's separate payload type remains a data object. Use ApplicationInterface instead when the type defines an access point (method contract, endpoint binding, client stub) through which a service is invoked, rather than the data exchanged through it. Use data-model ObjectSchema instead when the structure is a persisted entity or ORM model, or its shape is published as a schema (a JSON Schema, an OpenAPI component, a validation model that serializes to one); the object schema may then realize the data object. Evidence: A class, record, struct or dataclass consisting mainly of fields with getters/setters or immutable properties (e.g. Python @dataclass, Java record, Lombok @Data, C# record, TypeScript interface or type alias) and no injected dependencies.; The type appears as a parameter or return type in signatures of service, handler or function methods, e.g. names ending in Dto, Command, Request, Result, Model or ValueObject.; Instances are constructed, mapped (e.g. via a mapper or serializer) and mutated by other classes, while the type itself carries no ORM table mapping, OpenAPI schema annotation or UI binding that would place it in a more specific layer..","none":"None of the listed types fits this element well."}}}}

