```","questions":{"element_type":{"type":"choice","instructions":"This symbol belongs to the 'application' layer. Which element type within that layer best describes it? Choose 'none' if no listed type fits. The symbol is a Module; only the element types a Module can be are listed.","criteria":{"applicationcollaboration":"ApplicationCollaboration: An active structure element that names a grouping of two or more application components (or nested collaborations) which jointly perform collective behaviour. It is a logical or configured grouping of independently deployable parts, not a deployable unit or behaviour in its own right. Use ApplicationComponent instead when the symbol is a single modular, deployable unit, even if it internally composes sub-modules or calls other services. Use ApplicationInteraction instead when the symbol describes the joint behaviour itself, such as a message exchange, saga step sequence or protocol between components, rather than the group of participants. Use ApplicationProcess instead when the symbol is an ordered sequence of steps that achieves a result, even if those steps span several components. Use ApplicationService instead when the symbol is the externally exposed functionality the group offers rather than the group of participating components. Evidence: a saga or choreography definition (e.g. Temporal/Camunda workflow config, event-flow manifest) that enumerates several named participating services under one collective name; a docker-compose file, Helm umbrella chart or Kubernetes namespace that groups multiple separately built services as one named subsystem delivering a shared capability; an architecture document, ADR or C4 diagram boundary that names a set of cooperating services (e.g. 'checkout subsystem: cart, pricing, payment') as a unit.","applicationcomponent":"ApplicationComponent: An active structural application element that encapsulates a cohesive set of application behaviour and data behind its interfaces and can be built, deployed, or replaced as a unit, such as a service, module, package, or dependency-injected class with its own responsibility. It is the thing that performs behaviour, not the behaviour itself. Use ApplicationFunction instead when the symbol is a unit of behaviour, such as a method, function, or handler body, that a component performs, rather than the structural unit that owns that behaviour. Use ApplicationInterface instead when the symbol is only the access point through which a component's services are reached, such as a controller endpoint set, client stub, or exported port, rather than the implementing unit. Use ApplicationCollaboration instead when the symbol represents a grouping of several components that work together, such as a system-of-services or a composite deployment, rather than a single replaceable unit. Use DataObject instead when the symbol is a passive data structure, such as a DTO, record, or entity without behaviour of its own, that components read or write. Evidence: a module, package, or service directory with its own build manifest or deployment descriptor (package.json, pom.xml, go.mod, Dockerfile, Kubernetes Deployment); a class annotated or registered as a managed component in a DI container (@Component, @Service, @Injectable, Module providers) whose instances are injected into other classes; an entry in docker-compose, Helm charts, or an architecture/service catalogue (e.g. Backstage catalog-info.yaml) naming the unit as a separately deployable service or library.","applicationfunction":"ApplicationFunction: A unit of automated behavior performed by a single application component, grouped by the kind of work or capability it covers rather than by execution order or trigger, and invoked from inside the system rather than offered as a contract to external consumers. It may be an event handler that an ApplicationEvent triggers, such as a message listener or subscriber callback. Use ApplicationProcess instead when the behavior is an o